  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The forecast area will be located\nbetween a ridge of high pressure over the Rocky Mountains and an\nEastern Pacific trough of low pressure this afternoon. High\ntemperatures this afternoon will be two to four degrees above\nnormal for this time of year. The Eastern Pacific trough of low\npressure will approach the west coast of the United States\ntonight. An upper-level area of low pressure will form in the base\nof this trough near the central coast of California Sunday.\nElevated fire weather conditions are expected in parts of Eastern\nNevada Sunday due to south to southwest wind gusts near 40 mph and\nminimum relative humidity values as low as 10 percent. \n\nThe aforementioned cyclone aloft will slowly move southeastward\nacross Southern Nevada Monday, resulting in the likelihood of\nshowers in East Central Nevada Monday afternoon. A few strong\nthunderstorms are conceivable in most of Eastern Nevada Monday\nafternoon into Monday evening. The primary thunderstorm hazards\nwill be wind gusts near 45 mph and dangerous cloud to ground\nlightning strikes. A ridge of high pressure will build westward\nacross Northern Nevada Tuesday through Friday. A warming trend is\nanticipated Tuesday through Thursday. By Thursday afternoon,\nmaximum temperatures in Northern and Central Nevada will be six to\neight degrees above seasonal values.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence\nregarding elevated fire weather conditions in parts of Eastern\nNevada Sunday. Moderate forecast confidence in the possibility of\na few strong thunderstorms in most of Eastern Nevada Monday\nafternoon into Monday evening. Moderate forecast confidence\nregarding the likelihood of showers in East Central Nevada \nMonday. Low forecast confidence in a warming trend Tuesday through\nThursday. No deviations from the NBM model output.",
